      "observed": "The isolated CAN transceivers U10 and U11 have both of their isolated ground pins, GND1 and GND2, connected to the same system GND net.",
      "expected": "The specification describes two isolated CAN buses implemented through isolated CAN transceivers; the transceiver ground on the CAN side (GND2) should be isolated from the controller/system ground (GND1), not tied to the common GND net.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "U10.4(GND1,power_in) U10.5(GND2,power_in) U11.4(GND1,power_in) U11.5(GND2,power_in)"
        },
        {
          "source": "spec",
          "quote": "two isolated CAN buses (`CAN0`, `CAN1`), each driven by its own MCP2515 SPI CAN controller (`U8`, `U9`) through an isolated CAN transceiver (`U11`/companion)"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "The galvanic isolation intended for the CAN interfaces is defeated; the CAN transceivers behave as non-isolated devices, allowing ground loops and common-mode noise from external CAN connections to couple into the board.",
      "confidence": 0.72
    }
  ],
  "summary": "An anomaly is present: the isolated CAN transceivers U10 and U11 have both isolated-side grounds tied to the common system GND, contradicting the specified isolated CAN buses and defeating the intended galvanic isolation."
}

